  "id": "DrugBank_DB00376 - MONDO_14231",
  "verbalization": "Trihexyphenidyl, a muscarinic receptor antagonist, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Juvenile Onset Parkinson Disease 19a through a series of interconnected indications. The evidence suggests that Trihexyphenidyl may exert a role in the development of this condition by influencing the basal ganglia, a key brain region involved in motor control. This is supported by its association with Basal Ganglia Disease and Atypical Juvenile Parkinsonism, both of which are linked to the disease. The mechanism of action of Trihexyphenidyl, which involves blocking muscarinic receptors, may contribute to the pathophysiology of Juvenile Onset Parkinson Disease 19a by modulating neurotransmitter balance in the basal ganglia. The presence of overlapping indications between Trihexyphenidyl and other drugs like Amantadine and Opicapone further suggests a potential shared pathophysiological pathway. While the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated, the integrated evidence points to a possible role of Trihexyphenidyl in the pathogenesis of this disease, particularly through its effects on muscarinic receptors and their involvement in basal ganglia function.",
